\subsection{Reconstructed scoring and the query-only constant}

For $\gamma=2\langle\widehat p,\widehat r\rangle$, expand in real arithmetic:
\begin{align}
 \refineddist(q,x)
 &=\primarydist(q,x)+\|q'-\widehat r(x)\|_2^2+\gamma(x)
 \label{eq:5-refined-score}\\
 &=2\|q'\|_2^2+\|\widehat p+\widehat r\|_2^2-2\langle q',\widehat p+\widehat r\rangle\nonumber\\
 &=\|q'-\widehat p(x)-\widehat r(x)\|_2^2+\|q'\|_2^2.
 \label{eq:5-refined-constant}
\end{align}
The query constant preserves reconstructed-distance order \cite{jhq}.

Scalar scoring sums squared residual differences, then adds primary score/correction. Queries and fitting codebooks use shared memory.

\subsection{Mapping lanes to words and reducing partial sums}

For $\lrefine=L\in\{1,8,16,32\}$,
\[
 d_{\rm word}=32/B_R,\qquad
 \text{words of lane }a:\ a,a+L,a+2L,\ldots.
\]
A word holds four eight-bit or eight four-bit codes. Butterfly shuffles exchange and sum partial values between lanes;
lane zero writes the score.

\par\medskip\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\captionsetup{hypcap=false}
\centering
\input{variants/thesis2/figures/lane-refinement}
\captionof{figure}[Sixteen-lane residual refinement]{Sixteen-lane residual refinement.}
\label{fig:5-lane-refinement}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

Two sixteen-lane subgroups of a warp handle separate candidates. Local lane $a$ reads residual words $a,a+16,\ldots$; each subgroup reduces its partial sums and adds its candidate's primary score and correction. Warp lane~0 and lane~16 write the two scores separately.

Word-aligned rows and $d_{\rm word}\mid D_s$ keep words within codebooks. All lanes shuffle; invalid slots contribute zero, then output infinity. $L=16$ gives two candidates/warp.

L0 loads bytes; L1 loads words with the same one-lane accumulation. L8/L16/L32 trade shorter walks for shuffles/fewer concurrent candidates. FP32 association and locality matter.

\subsection{Final selection and the numerical boundary}

For at least $k$ finite valid survivors with unique IDs, each round computes
\[
 i^*=\arg\min_{i<c}(D_{H,i},\mathrm{ID}_i),\quad d^*=D_{H,i^*},\quad
 D_{H,i^*}\leftarrow+\infty
\]
using all slots and emits $(\mathrm{ID}_{i^*},d^*)$: exact retained-pool top-$k$, without thread quotas. Underfull pools may repeat the smallest valid ID at infinity; all-invalid pools return invalid IDs. Tests meet the finite-entry premise.

Primary ties use positions, final ties IDs. Exclusions/quantisation remain; lane FP32 association may move near ties.

For routed $U(q)$, the refinement multiplier sets \cite{jhq}
\begin{equation}
 c(\alpha)=\max\{k,\lceil\alpha k\rceil\},\qquad
 C_\alpha(q)=\operatorname{Top}^{\rm primary}_{\min\{c(\alpha),|U(q)|\}}U(q).
 \label{eq:6-budget}
\end{equation}
Analysis requires $|C_\alpha|\geq k$; underfull slots are sentinels. The budget changes retention; probe selection determines $U$.
Each tested budget is an experimental arm.

At $k=10$, the grid retains 20--2,000 survivors without retraining. Lower budgets save reads, not residual storage or previously reserved workspace.



Shading in \Cref{fig:5-query-path} marks the budget-dependent work. The routed candidates and number of primary scores are fixed, while admission and compaction within the scan kernel still depend on the budget. Sentinel padding fills refinement slots when candidates are insufficient.

At fixed batch/routing, write
\begin{equation}
 T(\alpha)=T_{\rm base}+T_{\rm select}(c(\alpha))+
 T_{\rm refine}(c(\alpha))+T_{\rm final}(c(\alpha)).
 \label{eq:6-cost-model}
\end{equation}
$T_{\rm base}$ covers transfers/rotation/routing/primary arithmetic (\cref{fig:5-query-path}). Selection/resource transitions make latency non-monotone; the rule minimises accepted budget (\cref{sec:7-budget-trace}).

Trials reuse the index but rerun full searches, including rotation/probing/primary scoring; calibration costs more than residual work alone.



\paragraph{Position among adaptive ANN methods.}
$\alpha$ serves the candidate-expansion role of Faiss's \texttt{k\_factor} and cuVS refinement \cite{faiss_refine,cuvs_refinement}. Alternatives use GT tuning \cite{faiss_autotune}, learned per-query stopping \cite{li_termination} or predicted recall \cite{darth}. Here unlabelled agreement chooses one reusable workload budget. Scores still use reconstructed vectors, unlike the cuVS source-vector refinement example.

